<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kirtipur</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kirtipur is an old Newar town on a ridge south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu">Kathmandu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prithvi-narayan-shah">Prithvi Narayan Shah</a> of Gorkha failed to take it in two assaults before capturing it after a long siege in the 1760s, punishing its defenders by cutting off their noses. Its fall opened the way to his conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu-valley">Kathmandu Valley</a> in 1768.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kirtipur.jpg" alt="Kirtipur" width="320" height="221" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Etter Studio · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kirtipur,_1950_-_1955.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After two failed assaults he took the hill town of <b>Kirtipur</b> in 1766, and in September 1768 his troops entered Kathmandu during the festival of Indra Jatra.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-nepal">Kingdom of Nepal · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Prithvi Narayan Shah, who took the throne in 1743, blockaded the Kathmandu Valley's trade, took the Kingdom of Makwanpur in 1762 and <b>Kirtipur</b> in 1767, and armed his soldiers with muskets.</blockquote><a href="/state/gorkha-kingdom">Gorkha Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
